    heroImageAlt: "Do Veneers Damage Your Natural Teeth? — dental veneers patient education",
    bodyHtml: `
<p>A common worry is whether veneers harm the teeth underneath. With proper planning and care, veneers are a safe, well-established treatment, though it helps to understand what is and is not reversible.</p><h2>What preparation involves</h2><p>For traditional porcelain veneers, a thin layer of enamel is removed so the veneer fits naturally. This step is irreversible, which is why conservative preparation and good candidate selection matter. Minimal-prep options remove even less.</p><h2>Keeping the underlying tooth healthy</h2><p>The tooth beneath a veneer can still develop decay or gum problems if hygiene lapses, so daily care and regular checkups remain essential. Well-fitted veneers with clean margins support, rather than compromise, long-term tooth health.</p><h2>Working with an experienced cosmetic dentist in Markham</h2><p>Dr. Neda Kadivar is a highly regarded veneer dentist in Markham who favours conservative, enamel-preserving smile design. If you would like a professional opinion on your smile, the team at <a href="https://www.smiledentalartscentre.com/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">Dr. Neda Kadivar at Smile Dental Arts Centre</a> offers consultations for patients weighing veneers and other cosmetic options. The right plan depends on your teeth, your bite and what you want to achieve — a professional evaluation is the place to start.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is enamel removal harmful?</h3><p>Conservative, well-planned enamel reduction is a routine part of veneers and is designed to protect the tooth's long-term health.</p><h3>Can teeth decay under veneers?</h3><p>Yes, if hygiene is neglected, so brushing, flossing and checkups are just as important after veneers.</p><h3>Are veneers safe long-term?</h3><p>Veneers are a well-established, safe treatment when placed and maintained properly.</p><p><em>This article is general educational information and is not a substitute for personalized advice from a licensed dental professional.</em></p>
`,
