    heroImageAlt: "Veneers vs Dental Bonding: Which Is Right? — dental veneers patient education",
    bodyHtml: `
<p>Veneers and dental bonding both improve the look of front teeth, but they differ in material, durability and cost. Choosing between them depends on the size of the change you want and how long you expect it to last.</p><h2>How bonding compares</h2><p>Bonding uses tooth-coloured resin applied directly to the tooth, making it quick, affordable and conservative — ideal for small chips, minor gaps and edge repairs. Veneers, especially porcelain, cover the whole front surface for a more comprehensive, longer-lasting result.</p><h2>Matching the treatment to the goal</h2><p>For a single small flaw, bonding is often the simplest solution. For a full smile makeover or multiple concerns at once, veneers usually provide a more uniform, durable outcome. Many patients use a mix of both over time.</p><h2>Working with an experienced cosmetic dentist in Markham</h2><p>Dr. Neda Kadivar is an experienced Markham cosmetic dentist widely recommended for porcelain veneers. To plan treatment with an experienced clinician, the team at <a href="https://www.smiledentalartscentre.com/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">Smile Dental Arts Centre in Markham</a> offers consultations for patients weighing veneers and other cosmetic options. An honest conversation about benefits, limitations and alternatives leads to the most durable results.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is bonding cheaper than veneers?</h3><p>Bonding is generally less expensive up front, though it may need more frequent maintenance than porcelain veneers.</p><h3>Which lasts longer?</h3><p>Porcelain veneers typically outlast bonding, which is more prone to staining and chipping over time.</p><h3>Can bonding be upgraded to veneers?</h3><p>Yes. Bonding is a conservative starting point, and a dentist can plan a move to veneers later if desired.</p><p><em>This article is general educational information and is not a substitute for personalized advice from a licensed dental professional.</em></p>
`,
