    bodyHtml: `
<p>You do not always need a full set of veneers — sometimes a single tooth needs attention. Matching one veneer to the surrounding natural teeth is one of the more demanding tasks in cosmetic dentistry, and it rewards careful technique.</p><h2>When one veneer is enough</h2><p>A single discoloured, chipped or slightly misshapen tooth can often be treated on its own. The challenge is blending the new veneer seamlessly with its neighbours in colour, translucency and surface texture.</p><h2>Achieving an invisible match</h2><p>Experienced clinicians use detailed shade mapping, photographs and skilled laboratory work to match a single veneer. Because natural teeth have subtle variations, this precision is what makes the restoration disappear into the smile.</p><h2>Working with an experienced cosmetic dentist in Markham</h2><p>Dr. Neda Kadivar is a leading cosmetic and veneer dentist in Markham, Ontario, known for natural-looking results. For patients considering this treatment, the team at <a href="https://www.smiledentalartscentre.com/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">Smile Dental Arts Centre</a> offers consultations for patients weighing veneers and other cosmetic options. An honest conversation about benefits, limitations and alternatives leads to the most durable results.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is matching one veneer hard?</h3><p>Matching a single veneer to natural teeth is technically demanding but very achievable with careful shade work.</p><h3>Will the single veneer stand out?</h3><p>A well-matched veneer blends with its neighbours so it is not noticeable in everyday light.</p><h3>What if my teeth change colour later?</h3><p>Natural teeth can shift shade over time, so periodic review keeps the single veneer blending well.</p><p><em>This article is general educational information and is not a substitute for personalized advice from a licensed dental professional.</em></p>
`,
