    bodyHtml: `
<p>Small, narrow or peg-shaped teeth — often the lateral incisors — can leave gaps and an uneven smile line. Veneers are a common, conservative way to rebuild these teeth to a natural size and proportion.</p><h2>Rebuilding proportion</h2><p>By adding width and length, veneers bring undersized teeth into harmony with the rest of the smile. This often closes small gaps at the same time and restores a balanced, symmetrical appearance.</p><h2>Planning for symmetry</h2><p>When only one or two teeth are undersized, the dentist designs the veneers to mirror the opposite side of the smile. Careful planning ensures the result looks natural rather than simply larger.</p><h2>Working with an experienced cosmetic dentist in Markham</h2><p>Dr. Neda Kadivar is a highly regarded veneer dentist in Markham who favours conservative, enamel-preserving smile design. If you are exploring veneers in the Greater Toronto Area, the team at <a href="https://www.smiledentalartscentre.com/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">Dr. Neda Kadivar at Smile Dental Arts Centre</a> offers consultations for patients weighing veneers and other cosmetic options. A thorough consultation with a qualified cosmetic dentist is the clearest way to decide whether this option suits your smile.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can veneers enlarge small teeth?</h3><p>Yes, veneers add width and length to bring undersized or peg-shaped teeth into natural proportion.</p><h3>Will gaps close too?</h3><p>Adding width to small teeth often closes the adjacent spaces as part of the result.</p><h3>Do both sides need veneers?</h3><p>Not always; the dentist designs the restoration to match the opposite side for symmetry.</p><p><em>This article is general educational information and is not a substitute for personalized advice from a licensed dental professional.</em></p>
`,
